%% Chapter 28: Attribute Grammars and Syntax-Directed Semantics
\label{ch:attribute-grammars}

\section{Introduction to Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:ag-intro}

Context-free grammars define the \emph{syntax} of a language---the set of valid strings. But syntax alone is insufficient for programming languages: we need to express \emph{context-sensitive} constraints such as type compatibility, variable declaration before use, and unique label names. \emph{Attribute grammars}, introduced by Donald Knuth in 1968, extend context-free grammars with attributes and semantic rules, providing a formal framework for syntax-directed semantics.

The key insight of attribute grammars is that semantic information can be associated with the nodes of a parse tree and propagated through the tree via \emph{synthesized} and \emph{inherited} attributes. This approach is particularly well suited to compiler implementation, where the compiler front end must perform tasks such as type checking, name resolution, and intermediate code generation---all of which require context-sensitive information that flows through the parse tree.

\begin{historical}
Donald Knuth introduced attribute grammars in his 1968 paper ``Semantics of Context-Free Languages,'' motivated by the need for a formal framework for syntax-directed translation. The concept was independently anticipated by work on macro processing and tree translation. The subsequent development of attribute grammars was driven by the desire for automatic compiler generation: if semantic rules could be specified as attributes, then tools like parser generators could automatically determine evaluation order and generate efficient code. Key contributors include Der{\u{r}} (1950s, on tree translation), Waite and Hamilton (1970s, on attribute evaluation algorithms), and Reps (1980s, on the Synthesizer Generator).
\end{historical}

\section{Attribute Grammars: Basic Concepts}
\label{sec:ag-basic}

\subsection{Motivation}
\label{sec:ag-motivation}

Consider the task of computing the value of an arithmetic expression. A context-free grammar defines the structure:

\begin{grammar}{Arithmetic Expressions}
  E & \longrightarrow E + T \\
    & \mid T \\
  T & \longrightarrow T \times F \\
    & \mid F \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( E ) \\
    & \mid \textbf{num}
\end{grammar}

This grammar defines which strings are valid expressions, but it does not tell us how to compute the value of an expression. We need additional information---\emph{attributes} attached to the grammar symbols and \emph{semantic rules} that define how attributes are computed.

\subsection{Definition of Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:ag-definition}

\begin{definition}[Attribute Grammar]
\label{def:attribute-grammar}
An \textbf{attribute grammar} is a context-free grammar $G = (N, T, P, S)$ augmented with:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item For each grammar symbol $X$ (terminal or nonterminal), a finite set of \textbf{attributes} $A(X) = S(X) \cup I(X)$, where $S(X)$ is the set of \textbf{synthesized} attributes and $I(X)$ is the set of \textbf{inherited} attributes.
  \item For each production $p \in P$ of the form $X_0 \to X_1 X_2 \cdots X_n$, a set of \textbf{semantic rules} of the form:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item $X_i.a = f_p(X_0.b_0, X_1.b_1, \ldots, X_n.b_n)$ for synthesized attributes $a \in S(X_i)$
      \item $X_i.a = f_p(X_0.b_0, X_1.b_1, \ldots, X_n.b_n)$ for inherited attributes $a \in I(X_i)$
    \end{itemize}
    where each $f_p$ is a function defined on the attributes of the grammar symbols in the production.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The distinction between synthesized and inherited attributes is fundamental:

\begin{definition}[Synthesized Attribute]
\label{def:synthesized-attribute}
A \textbf{synthesized} attribute of a grammar symbol $X_i$ in a production $X_0 \to X_1 \cdots X_n$ is an attribute whose value is computed from attributes of $X_i$ itself and attributes of the symbols $X_0, X_1, \ldots, X_n$ (including $X_i$'s own children in the parse tree). Synthesized attributes flow \emph{upward} in the parse tree.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Inherited Attribute]
\label{def:inherited-attribute}
An \textbf{inherited} attribute of a grammar symbol $X_i$ in a production $X_0 \to X_1 \cdots X_n$ is an attribute whose value is computed from attributes of $X_0$ (the parent) and attributes of the sibling symbols $X_j$ ($j \neq i$). Inherited attributes flow \emph{downward} and \emph{sideways} in the parse tree.
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
The restriction on attribute dependencies is not merely technical---it ensures that attribute evaluation is well-defined. A synthesized attribute of $X_i$ can depend on attributes of $X_i$'s children and $X_i$ itself. An inherited attribute of $X_i$ can depend on attributes of $X_i$'s parent and siblings. These restrictions prevent circular dependencies in many practical grammars and ensure that attributes can be evaluated in a single pass (or a bounded number of passes) over the parse tree.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Example: Expression Evaluation}
\label{sec:ag-example-eval}

\begin{grammar}{Arithmetic Expressions with Attributes}
  E & \longrightarrow E_1 + T \qquad & E.\textbf{val} = E_1.\textbf{val} + T.\textbf{val} \\
    & \mid T \qquad & E.\textbf{val} = T.\textbf{val} \\
  T & \longrightarrow T_1 \times F \qquad & T.\textbf{val} = T_1.\textbf{val} \times F.\textbf{val} \\
    & \mid F \qquad & T.\textbf{val} = F.\textbf{val} \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( E ) \qquad & F.\textbf{val} = E.\textbf{val} \\
    & \mid \textbf{num} \qquad & F.\textbf{val} = \textbf{num}.\textbf{lexval}
\end{grammar}

All attributes in this grammar are synthesized: the value of each expression is computed from the values of its subexpressions, flowing upward through the parse tree. The attribute \textbf{val} is synthesized for $E$, $T$, and $F$, while \textbf{lexval} is a lexical attribute of the terminal \textbf{num}.

\section{Dependency Graphs and Evaluation Order}
\label{sec:dependency-graphs}

\subsection{Dependency Graphs}
\label{sec:dep-graphs}

For a given parse tree, the \emph{dependency graph} (or \emph{attribute dependency graph}) captures the data flow between attribute instances.

\begin{definition}[Dependency Graph]
\label{def:dependency-graph}
Given a parse tree $T$ of an attribute grammar, the \textbf{dependency graph} $D(T)$ is a directed graph where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The nodes are the attribute instances: for each node $n$ in $T$ labeled with grammar symbol $X$, and each attribute $a \in A(X)$, there is a node $n.a$ in $D(T)$.
  \item There is a directed edge from $m.b$ to $n.a$ if the semantic rule for $n.a$ depends on $m.b$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Well-Defined Attribute Grammar]
\label{def:well-defined-ag}
An attribute grammar is \textbf{well-defined} (or \textbf{non-circular}) if for every parse tree $T$, the dependency graph $D(T)$ is a directed acyclic graph (DAG).
\end{definition}

If the dependency graph is a DAG, the attributes can be evaluated by any topological sort of the graph. If the graph contains a cycle, the attributes are circular and cannot be evaluated.

\subsection{Evaluation Order}
\label{sec:evaluation-order}

\begin{definition}[Topological Evaluation Order]
\label{def:topological-eval}
A \textbf{topological evaluation order} for the dependency graph $D(T)$ is a linear ordering of the nodes such that if there is an edge from $m.b$ to $n.a$, then $m.b$ appears before $n.a$ in the ordering.
\end{definition}

For a non-circular attribute grammar, a topological evaluation order always exists. The challenge is to find an evaluation order that works for \emph{all} parse trees, not just a specific one.

\begin{definition}[Ordered Attribute Grammar]
\label{def:ordered-ag}
An attribute grammar is \textbf{ordered} if there exists a single evaluation scheme (a fixed ordering of attribute evaluations for each production) that is consistent with all dependency graphs.
\end{definition}

Not all non-circular attribute grammars are ordered. For ordered grammars, a single pass over the parse tree (visiting nodes in a fixed order) suffices to evaluate all attributes.

\subsection{Evaluation Algorithms}
\label{sec:eval-algorithms}

Several algorithms exist for evaluating attributes of a parse tree:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Tree traversal methods}: For $S$-attributed grammars (only synthesized attributes), a single bottom-up traversal suffices. For $L$-attributed grammars, a single top-down traversal suffices.
  \item \textbf{Rule-by-rule method}: Evaluate attributes in a fixed order for each production, repeating passes until all attributes are computed.
  \item \textbf{Derivative method}: Construct a derivative grammar that encodes the evaluation order, then evaluate using a single tree traversal.
  \item \textbf{Topological sort on demand}: For each parse tree, construct the dependency graph and evaluate attributes in topological order.
\end{itemize}

\section{Classes of Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:ag-classes}

\subsection{S-Attributed Grammars}
\label{sec:s-attributed}

\begin{definition}[S-Attributed Grammar]
\label{def:s-attributed}
An attribute grammar is \textbf{S-attributed} if all attributes are synthesized (no inherited attributes).
\end{definition}

S-attributed grammars can be evaluated during bottom-up parsing (e.g., LR parsing) by evaluating attributes as reductions are performed. The evaluation order is a post-order traversal of the parse tree.

\subsection{L-Attributed Grammars}
\label{sec:l-attributed}

\begin{definition}[L-Attributed Grammar]
\label{def:l-attributed}
An attribute grammar is \textbf{L-attributed} if each inherited attribute of $X_i$ in a production $X_0 \to X_1 \cdots X_n$ depends only on:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Inherited attributes of $X_0$ (the parent)
  \item Synthesized or inherited attributes of $X_j$ for $j < i$ (left siblings)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

L-attributed grammars can be evaluated during top-down parsing (e.g., LL parsing) by evaluating attributes as the parse tree is constructed from left to right. The left-to-right restriction ensures that all required attributes are available when needed.

\begin{example}[L-Attributed Grammar for Type Checking]
\label{ex:l-attributed-types}
Consider a grammar for type declarations:
\begin{grammar}{Type Declarations}
  D & \longrightarrow T\; L \qquad & L.\textbf{type} = T.\textbf{type} \\
  T & \longrightarrow \textbf{int} \qquad & T.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int} \\
    & \mid \textbf{float} \qquad & T.\textbf{type} = \textbf{float} \\
  L & \longrightarrow L_1 , \textbf{id} \qquad & L_1.\textbf{type} = L.\textbf{type}; \quad \textbf{addtype}(\textbf{id}.\textbf{entry}, L.\textbf{type}) \\
    & \mid \textbf{id} \qquad & \textbf{addtype}(\textbf{id}.\textbf{entry}, L.\textbf{type})
\end{grammar}

The attribute \textbf{type} is inherited by $L$ from $T$ (its parent in the production for $D$) and then passed down through the list. The function \textbf{addtype} enters the type into the symbol table.
\end{example}

\subsection{Non-Circular Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:noncircular}

The most general class of practical attribute grammars is the class of \emph{non-circular} attribute grammars---those whose dependency graphs are acyclic for all parse trees. This class includes both S-attributed and L-attributed grammars as special cases.

\begin{theorem}[Non-Circularity Test]
\label{thm:noncircular-test}
There exists an algorithm to test whether an attribute grammar is non-circular. The algorithm constructs a ``higher-order'' dependency graph that over-approximates the dependency graphs of all parse trees and checks it for cycles.
\end{theorem}

The test is based on the observation that if the attribute grammar is circular, then there exists a parse tree whose dependency graph contains a cycle, and this cycle can be detected by analyzing the dependency patterns of individual productions.

\section{Circular Attribute Detection}
\label{sec:circular-detection}

\subsection{The Problem of Circularity}
\label{sec:circularity-problem}

An attribute grammar is circular if there exists a parse tree whose dependency graph contains a cycle. Circularity means that some attribute values cannot be determined---they depend on themselves, directly or indirectly.

\begin{example}[Circular Attribute Grammar]
\label{ex:circular-ag}
Consider a grammar with the production $A \to B\; C$ and semantic rules:
\[
B.i = C.s \qquad C.i = B.s
\]
If $B.s$ depends on $B.i$ and $C.s$ depends on $C.i$, then we have a circular dependency: $B.i \to C.s \to C.i \to B.s \to B.i$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Detecting Circularity}
\label{sec:detect-circularity}

The standard algorithm for detecting circularity works by constructing a \emph{partitioned dependency graph} for each production and then composing these graphs across all possible parse trees.

\begin{definition}[Production Dependency Graph]
\label{def:prod-dep-graph}
For a production $p: X_0 \to X_1 \cdots X_n$, the \textbf{production dependency graph} has nodes for each attribute instance of $X_0, \ldots, X_n$ and edges representing direct dependencies in the semantic rules.
\end{definition}

The algorithm then checks whether the composition of production dependency graphs over all possible parse trees can create a cycle. This is done by computing a fixed point: starting with the direct dependencies, iteratively adding edges that arise from the composition of productions, until no new edges are added or a cycle is detected.

\subsection{Handling Conditional Circularity}
\label{sec:conditional-circularity}

In practice, some attribute grammars are circular for certain parse trees but not others. For example, a grammar for type inference might have circular dependencies only for programs with recursive type definitions. Handling such cases requires:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Iterative evaluation}: Evaluate attributes repeatedly until a fixed point is reached (or divergence is detected).
  \item \textbf{Lazy evaluation}: Evaluate attributes on demand, detecting cycles at evaluation time.
  \item \textbf{Relaxed attribute grammars}: Allow circular attributes but require that they converge to a fixed point.
\end{itemize}

\section{Applications of Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:ag-applications}

\subsection{Type Checking}
\label{sec:ag-type-checking}

Type checking is one of the most important applications of attribute grammars. The type of each expression is a synthesized attribute, computed from the types of its subexpressions. Type environments are inherited attributes, passed down from declarations to uses.

\begin{grammar}{Type Checking with Attributes}
  E & \longrightarrow E_1 + E_2 \qquad & E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{if } E_1.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int} \land E_2.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int} \textbf{ then int else error} \\
    & \longrightarrow E_1 = E_2 \qquad & E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{if } E_1.\textbf{type} = E_2.\textbf{type} \textbf{ then bool else error} \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{id} \qquad & E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{lookup}(\textbf{id}.\textbf{entry}, \Gamma) \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{num} \qquad & E.\textbf{type} = \textbf{int}
\end{grammar}

\subsection{Code Generation}
\label{sec:ag-code-gen}

Intermediate code generation can be specified as an attribute grammar where the synthesized attribute is the generated code for each construct:

\begin{grammar}{Code Generation with Attributes}
  E & \longrightarrow E_1 + E_2 \qquad & E.\textbf{code} = E_1.\textbf{code} \concat E_2.\textbf{code} \concat \textbf{gen}(\texttt{ADD}) \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{id} \qquad & E.\textbf{code} = \textbf{gen}(\texttt{LOAD } \textbf{id}.\textbf{addr}) \\
    & \longrightarrow \textbf{num} \qquad & E.\textbf{code} = \textbf{gen}(\texttt{LOADI } \textbf{num}.\textbf{val})
\end{grammar}

\subsection{Name Resolution}
\label{sec:ag-name-resolution}

Name resolution---binding identifiers to their declarations---is naturally expressed using inherited attributes for the environment and synthesized attributes for the resolved bindings.

\subsection{Semantic Analysis in General}
\label{sec:ag-semantic-analysis}

Attribute grammars provide a general framework for semantic analysis, encompassing:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Scope analysis and symbol table construction
  \item Type checking and type inference
  \item Flow analysis (def-use chains, live variable analysis)
  \item Intermediate code generation
  \item Constant folding and simple optimizations
\end{itemize}

\section{Comparison with Operational Semantics}
\label{sec:ag-vs-opsem}

\subsection{Relationship to Structural Operational Semantics}
\label{sec:ag-vs-sos}

Attribute grammars and structural operational semantics (SOS) both provide formal frameworks for specifying the semantics of programming languages. However, they differ in several important respects:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{p{3.5cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Attribute Grammars} & \textbf{Operational Semantics} \\
\hline
Basis & Parse trees & Transition systems \\
Semantics & Decorated trees & State transitions \\
Compositionality & Tree-structured & Rule-based \\
Evaluation & Batch (all attributes) & Step-by-step \\
Termination & Guaranteed (if non-circular) & May not terminate \\
Implementation & Direct (tree traversal) & Abstract machine \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Comparison of attribute grammars and operational semantics}
\label{tab:ag-vs-opsem}
\end{table}

\subsection{Complementary Strengths}
\label{sec:ag-complementary}

Attribute grammars excel at specifying \emph{static} semantic properties---those that can be determined by examining the program text without executing it. Operational semantics excels at specifying \emph{dynamic} behavior---how programs execute step by step.

In a complete language definition, both approaches are often used: attribute grammars for static semantics (type checking, scope analysis) and operational semantics for dynamic semantics (evaluation, execution).

\section{Attribute Grammar Implementations}
\label{sec:ag-implementations}

\subsection{The Synthesizer Generator}
\label{sec:synthesizer-generator}

Reps and Teitelbaum's Synthesizer Generator (1984) was the first comprehensive environment for attribute grammar-based language definition. It provided:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Automatic evaluation order determination
  \item Incremental attribute evaluation (re-evaluating only affected attributes when the program changes)
  \item Interactive language development
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Modern Attribute Grammar Systems}
\label{sec:modern-ag}

Modern systems for attribute grammar-based language development include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{JastAdd}: A Java-based system for attribute grammar specifications, used in compiler construction courses.
  \item \textbf{UUAG} (Utrecht University Attribute Grammar): An extension of Haskell with attribute grammar constructs.
  \item \textbf{Kiama}: A Scala library for attribute grammar-like processing of tree structures.
  \item \textbf{Silver}: An attribute grammar system for composing language specifications (used in the AbleC extensible C compiler).
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:ag-summary}

Attribute grammars provide a powerful and practical framework for syntax-directed semantics. By extending context-free grammars with attributes and semantic rules, they enable the specification of context-sensitive properties in a declarative, compositional manner. The distinction between synthesized and inherited attributes captures the two fundamental directions of information flow in a parse tree, and the dependency graph provides a formal basis for determining evaluation order.

The theory of attribute grammars continues to evolve, with ongoing research in areas such as higher-order attribute grammars, reference attributes, adaptive attribute grammars, and the integration of attribute grammars with functional programming languages.

\begin{historical}
The development of attribute grammars from Knuth's 1968 paper to modern language workbenches spans over five decades. Key milestones include: Knuth's original formulation (1968); the development of evaluation algorithms by Waite, Hamilton, and Der{\u{r}} (1970s); the classification into S-attributed, L-attributed, and non-circular grammars (1970s--1980s); the Synthesizer Generator (Reps and Teitelbaum, 1984); higher-order attribute grammars (Vogt, Swierstra, and Kuiper, 1989); reference attributes (Paakki, 1995); and modern systems like JastAdd (Ekman and Hedin, 2007) and Silver (Viera et al., 2018).
\end{historical}

