\section{Circular Attributes and Iterative Evaluation}
\label{sec:circular-attributes}

\subsection{Benign Circularity}
\label{sec:benign-circularity}

While Knuth's original definition requires non-circularity, many practical attribute grammars exhibit \emph{benign circularity}: the dependency graph may contain cycles for certain parse trees, but the attributes still converge to a well-defined fixed point through iterative evaluation.

\begin{definition}[Benign Circularity]
\label{def:benign-circularity}
An attribute grammar is \textbf{benignly circular} if for every parse tree, the iterative evaluation of attributes (repeatedly applying semantic rules until no attribute changes) converges to a unique fixed point in a finite number of steps.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Type Inference with Circular Attributes]
\label{ex:circular-type-inference}
Consider a simple type inference system where the type of a recursive function depends on the type of its body, which in turn depends on the type of the function itself:
\begin{grammar}{Circular Type Inference}
  E & \longrightarrow \textbf{fun } f(x) = E_1 \qquad & \begin{array}{l} f.\textbf{type} = f.\textbf{type} \to E_1.\textbf{type} \\ E.\textbf{type} = f.\textbf{type} \end{array} \\
    & \longrightarrow E_1\; E_2 \qquad & E.\textbf{type} = \text{result\_type}(E_1.\textbf{type}, E_2.\textbf{type})
\end{grammar}
The attribute $f.\textbf{type}$ appears on both sides of its defining equation---a circular dependency. However, iterative evaluation starting from $\textbf{unknown}$ converges to the correct type through successive approximations.
\end{example}

\subsection{Iterative Evaluation Algorithm}
\label{sec:iterative-eval}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Evaluate attributes with potential circularity}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Initialize all attributes to $\bottom$ (least defined value)
\Repeat
  \State $\text{changed} \gets \text{false}$
  \For{each node $n$ in the parse tree}
    \For{each attribute $a$ of $n$}
      \State $v_{\text{old}} \gets n.a$
      \State $n.a \gets f_p(\text{dependencies of } n.a)$
      \If{$n.a \neq v_{\text{old}}$}
        \State $\text{changed} \gets \text{true}$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\Until{$\text{changed} = \text{false}$ or max iterations exceeded}
\State \textbf{return} attribute values
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence of Iterative Evaluation]
\label{thm:iterative-convergence}
If all semantic functions are \emph{monotone} with respect to a partial order on attribute values (where $\bottom \sqsubseteq v$ for all $v$), and the attribute domain is a \emph{finite-height lattice}, then iterative evaluation converges to the least fixed point.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The attribute values form a finite-height lattice. Each iteration either increases some attribute value (moving up in the lattice) or reaches a fixed point. Since the lattice has finite height, the process must terminate. By the Knaster--Tarski theorem, the result is the least fixed point.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Demand-Driven Evaluation}
\label{sec:demand-driven}

An alternative to iterative evaluation is \emph{demand-driven} (lazy) evaluation: attributes are computed only when needed, and circular dependencies are detected at evaluation time.

\begin{definition}[Demand-Driven Evaluation]
\label{def:demand-driven}
In \textbf{demand-driven evaluation}, each attribute is computed on demand. When an attribute is requested:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item If its value has already been computed, return it.
  \item If it is currently being computed (on the evaluation stack), a cycle is detected.
  \item Otherwise, mark it as ``being computed,'' evaluate its dependencies recursively, and store the result.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Demand-driven evaluation is implemented in systems like JastAdd using a technique called \emph{rewritable attributes} with cycle detection.

\section{Higher-Order Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:higher-order-ag}

\subsection{Motivation}
\label{sec:hoag-motivation}

Standard attribute grammars compute values (numbers, strings, types) as attributes. \emph{Higher-order attribute grammars} (Vogt, Swierstra, Kuiper, 1989) allow attributes to be \emph{trees}---that is, an attribute can itself be a parse tree that has attributes.

\begin{definition}[Higher-Order Attribute]
\label{def:higher-order-attr}
A \textbf{higher-order attribute} is an attribute whose value is a decorated parse tree. The tree has its own attributes, which can be accessed through the higher-order attribute.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Macro Expansion as Higher-Order Attributes]
\label{ex:macro-hoag}
Consider a language with macros. A macro invocation is a tree node whose \textbf{expanded} attribute is the parse tree of the macro expansion. The expanded tree has its own attributes (type, value, etc.) that can be accessed:
\begin{grammar}{Macro Expansion}
  E & \longrightarrow \textbf{macro}(args) \qquad & E.\textbf{expanded} = \text{expand}(E.\textbf{macro\_def}, args) \\
    & & E.\textbf{type} = E.\textbf{expanded}.\textbf{type} \\
    & & E.\textbf{value} = E.\textbf{expanded}.\textbf{value}
\end{grammar}
The attribute \textbf{expanded} is a higher-order attribute: its value is a parse tree that can be further decorated with attributes.
\end{example}

\subsection{Semantics of Higher-Order Attributes}
\label{sec:hoag-semantics}

Higher-order attributes introduce the possibility of \emph{infinite} trees (a macro that expands to itself). The semantics must handle this through a fixed-point construction:

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-Point Semantics for HOAG]
\label{thm:hoag-fixed-point}
The semantics of a higher-order attribute grammar is given by the least fixed point of a continuous function on the domain of (possibly infinite) decorated trees. If the grammar is \emph{productive} (each expansion step produces a tree that is ``larger'' in some sense), the fixed point is a finite tree.
\end{theorem}

\section{Reference Attributes}
\label{sec:reference-attributes}

\subsection{The Need for References}
\label{sec:ref-need}

Many semantic analyses require building \emph{cross-references} between different parts of the parse tree. For example, a use of a variable must reference its declaration. \emph{Reference attributes} (Paakki, 1995) allow an attribute to hold a reference to another node in the parse tree.

\begin{definition}[Reference Attribute]
\label{def:reference-attr}
A \textbf{reference attribute} is an attribute whose value is a reference (pointer) to another node in the parse tree. The referenced node can be accessed, and its attributes can be queried.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Name Resolution with Reference Attributes]
\label{ex:ref-attr-names}
\begin{grammar}{Name Resolution}
  E & \longrightarrow \textbf{id} \qquad & E.\textbf{decl} = \text{lookup}(\textbf{id}.\textbf{name}, \Gamma) \\
    & & E.\textbf{type} = E.\textbf{decl}.\textbf{type}
\end{grammar}
The attribute $E.\textbf{decl}$ is a reference attribute pointing to the declaration node of the identifier. The type of the expression is obtained by accessing the type attribute of the declaration.
\end{example}

\subsection{Reference Attributes and AST Navigation}
\label{sec:ref-ast-nav}

Reference attributes enable direct navigation of the abstract syntax tree, bypassing the tree structure imposed by the grammar. This is particularly useful for:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Symbol table construction}: Declarations reference their scopes; uses reference their declarations.
  \item \textbf{Control flow graphs}: Statement nodes reference their successors and predecessors.
  \item \textbf{Data flow analysis}: Definition--use chains link definitions to their uses.
\end{itemize}

\section{Evaluation Strategies: A Comprehensive Comparison}
\label{sec:eval-strategies-comparison}

\subsection{Taxonomy of Evaluation Strategies}
\label{sec:eval-taxonomy}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{3cm}p{3cm}p{3cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Strategy} & \textbf{Passes} & \textbf{Circularity} & \textbf{Incremental} \\
\hline
Tree walk & 1--many & No & No \\
Rule-by-rule & $O(|A|)$ & No & No \\
Topological sort & 1 & No & No \\
Iterative & Many & Benign & Partial \\
Demand-driven & On demand & Detected & Yes \\
Higher-order & Fixed point & Productive & Partial \\
