\subsection{Incremental Attribute Evaluation}
\label{sec:incremental-eval}

When a program is edited (e.g., in an IDE), only a small portion of the parse tree changes. \emph{Incremental attribute evaluation} re-evaluates only the attributes affected by the change.

\begin{definition}[Change Propagation]
\label{def:change-propagation}
Given a modified parse tree $T'$ and the dependency graph $D(T)$ of the original tree $T$:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the attributes whose values may have changed (those that depend on the modified nodes).
  \item Re-evaluate only those attributes, propagating changes through the dependency graph.
  \item Stop when no more attributes change.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Incremental Efficiency]
\label{thm:incremental-efficiency}
If the modification affects $k$ nodes and the maximum number of attributes that depend on any single node is $d$, then incremental evaluation takes $O(k \cdot d)$ time in the worst case, compared to $O(|T| \cdot |A|)$ for a full re-evaluation.
\end{theorem}

The Synthesizer Generator (Reps and Teitelbaum, 1984) was the first system to implement incremental attribute evaluation, achieving interactive response times for language-based editors.

\section{Formal Properties of Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:ag-formal-properties}

\subsection{The Complexity of Non-Circularity Testing}
\label{sec:complexity-noncircular}

\begin{theorem}[Complexity of Non-Circularity]
\label{thm:complexity-nc}
Testing whether an attribute grammar is non-circular is \textbf{PSPACE-complete}. The problem remains PSPACE-hard even for attribute grammars with only synthesized and inherited attributes (no higher-order or reference attributes).
\end{theorem}

Despite the theoretical complexity, practical non-circularity tests work well for most attribute grammars encountered in practice. The test constructs an over-approximation of the dependency graphs and checks it for cycles.

\subsection{Absolutely Non-Circular Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:absolutely-nc}

\begin{definition}[Absolutely Non-Circular]
\label{def:absolutely-nc}
An attribute grammar is \textbf{absolutely non-circular} if it is non-circular for \emph{every} parse tree, including those that are not derivable from the start symbol. This is a stronger condition than non-circularity (which only considers valid parse trees).
\end{definition}

Absolutely non-circular grammars can be tested in polynomial time, making them attractive for practical tools.

\subsection{Attribute Grammar Transformations}
\label{sec:ag-transformations}

Several transformations can convert between different classes of attribute grammars:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{S-attributed to L-attributed}: Any S-attributed grammar is trivially L-attributed (no inherited attributes to constrain).
  \item \textbf{L-attributed to S-attributed}: An L-attributed grammar can be converted to an S-attributed grammar by converting inherited attributes to synthesized attributes, at the cost of duplicating information in the parse tree.
  \item \textbf{Non-circular to ordered}: Not all non-circular grammars can be converted to ordered grammars. The conversion may require grammar restructuring.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[Equivalence of AG Classes]
\label{thm:ag-equivalence}
Every non-circular attribute grammar can be simulated by a higher-order attribute grammar, and every higher-order attribute grammar can be simulated by a non-circular attribute grammar with reference attributes. The various classes of attribute grammars are thus equivalent in expressive power, differing only in convenience and efficiency.
\end{theorem}
